{% callout type="danger" title="Think FAST and call 999" %}
- **F**ace: has their face dropped on one side? Can they smile?
- **A**rms: can they lift both arms and keep them there?
- **S**peech: is their speech slurred or muddled?
- **T**ime: call **999** straight away if you see any of these signs

Other signs include sudden loss of sight, sudden weakness or numbness, dizziness, loss of balance and a sudden, severe headache.
{% /callout %}

A **stroke** happens when the blood supply to part of the brain is cut off. Without blood, brain cells start to die. There are around **100,000 strokes** in the UK each year.

## Types of stroke

- **ischaemic stroke**, caused by a blood clot blocking an artery. This is the most common type
- **haemorrhagic stroke**, caused by bleeding in or around the brain
- **transient ischaemic attack (TIA)** or mini stroke, where the blood supply is blocked for a short time and symptoms go within 24 hours

## Effects of a stroke

The effects depend on which part of the brain was affected. They can include:

- **weakness or paralysis** on one side of the body
- problems with **speech and language** (aphasia) or swallowing
- **tiredness** (fatigue), which is very common
- problems with **memory, thinking and concentration**
- changes to **sight**
- **emotional changes**, such as depression, anxiety or mood swings

## What raises the risk?

High blood pressure, atrial fibrillation, smoking, diabetes, high cholesterol, being overweight, drinking too much alcohol and not being active. Most strokes can be prevented.

Read about [stroke treatment and rehabilitation](/conditions/heart-and-circulation/stroke/treatment/).
